BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20260706T000000Z
DTEND:20260706T020000Z
LOCATION:Auer Hall
SUMMARY:Saxophone Academy Faculty Concert – Otis Murphy\, saxophone\; H
 aruko Murphy\, piano\; with Paul Lorenz\, saxophone
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  Ralph Martino: A Gershwin Fantasy (1991)C. T. Smi
 th: Fantasia (1983)Ryota Ishikawa: The Goddess and the Hourglass (2018)Ty
 ree Nesbitt: Until Next Time (2025)Borne/Meylan/Roth: Fantaisie brillante
  on Themes from Bizet's "Carmen" (1880)About the ArtistsOtis Murphy is pr
 ofessor of saxophone at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music\, w
 here he joined the faculty at age 28\, becoming one of the youngest profe
 ssors in the school's history. Recognized internationally for his artistr
 y\, he has performed in more than 25 countries across four continents\, a
 ppearing on distinguished stages including Carnegie Hall and Merkin Hall 
 in New York\, Casals Hall in Tokyo\, and the Palau de la Música in Valen
 cia. Murphy is a laureate in several national and international competiti
 ons\, including the Adolphe Sax International Saxophone Competition and t
 he Jean‑Marie Londeix International Saxophone Competition. His discogra
 phy includes numerous acclaimed recordings\, most notably his contributio
 n to Pacifica Quartet's Contemporary Voices\, featuring Ellen Taaffe Zwil
 ich's Quintet for Alto Saxophone and String Quartet\, which won a Grammy 
 Award for Best Chamber Music/Small Ensemble Performance. Internationally 
 respected as a pedagogue\, Murphy has led guest teaching engagements and 
 master classes at major institutions such as the Paris Conservatory\, the
  Amsterdam Conservatory\, the Cologne Academy of Music\, the Royal Colleg
 e of Music in London\, and the Hamamatsu International Wind Instrument Ac
 ademy. His students have garnered significant recognition\, earning award
 s in major competitions and holding professional appointments worldwide a
 s performers\, educators\, and members of premier military ensembles. Mur
 phy lives in Bloomington\, Indiana\, with his wife\, pianist Haruko Murph
 y. Together they share a vibrant performing partnership and a joyful fami
 ly life as devoted parents to six children.  Born in Japan\, Haruko Murph
 y moved with her family to San José\, California\, at age 14. She earned
  both the Bachelor of Music and Master of Music degrees in Piano Performa
 nce\, each with Highest Distinction\, from the Indiana University Jacobs 
 School of Music\, where she studied under Distinguished Professor Menahem
  Pressler. She later continued her training at the Sweelinck Conservatori
 um in Amsterdam as a pupil of Jan Huizing. A prizewinning solo pianist ea
 rly in her career\, Murphy now enjoys an active life as a collaborative p
 ianist and chamber musician. She specializes in the repertoire of the cla
 ssical saxophone and has performed in concert with many internationally r
 enowned artists\, including Jean‑Yves Fourmeau\, Arno Bornkamp\, the Ma
 sato Kumoi Saxophone Quartet\, and numerous others\, as well as her husba
 nd\, Otis Murphy. She is a frequent collaborative pianist at the Jacobs S
 chool.  Originally from Palisade\, Colorado\, Paul Lorenz currently lives
  in Fort Wayne\, Indiana\, where he is lecturer of saxophone and assistan
 t director of bands at Purdue University Fort Wayne (PFW). Lorenz is purs
 uing a Doctor of Music degree in Saxophone Performance at the Indiana Uni
 versity Jacobs School of Music\, where he served as associate instructor 
 of saxophone. He previously earned a Performer Diploma in Solo Performanc
 e at the Jacobs School and Master of Music and Bachelor of Music degrees\
 , respectively\, from the University of Missouri—Kansas City and the Un
 iversity of Colorado at Boulder. His primary teachers include Otis Murphy
 \, Zachary Shemon\, and Tom Myer. Lorenz is active as both a solo perform
 er and chamber musician and also teaches locally in the Fort Wayne area. 
 He was recently featured as a soloist with the PFW Symphonic Wind Ensembl
 e on its December concert as well as at the 2023 IMEA Conference in Fort 
 Wayne. He toured as soloist with the ensemble in Europe in May 2023. He w
 as featured as guest soloist with the South Shore Orchestra in a premier 
 of a piece by George Kontogiorgos in summer 2024. In addition to his work
  at PFW\, he is the on-call saxophonist for the Fort Wayne Philharmonic O
 rchestra. Lorenz is the alto saxophonist in Soma Quartet\, which formed a
 t the Jacobs School of Music in fall 2016. Soma has placed highly in nume
 rous competitions including the Fischoff National Chamber Music Competiti
 on\, Plowman Chamber Music Competition\, Music Teachers National Associat
 ion Competition\, NOLA Emerging Artists Chamber Competition (2021)\, Clas
 sics Alive Young Artist Competition\, and the Chicago Woodwind Ensemble C
 ompetition. Soma has performed recitals at the International Navy Band Sy
 mposium\, the North American Saxophone Alliance\, and was featured as par
 t of a WFIU NPR broadcast in 2018.
