SUMMARY:Saxophone Academy Faculty Concert – Paul Lorenz\, saxophone\; A
 ngela Space\, saxophone\; Haruko Murphy\, piano\; with Otis Murphy\, saxo
 phone
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  A. Marcello/Pittel: Oboe Concerto in C Minor\, S.
 Z799Piazzolla/Isoda/Sugawa: Selections from "Histoire du tango" (1985)Bea
 ch/Duncan: Romance\, Op. 23 (1893)Ryota Ishikawa: Blooming Ireland (2013)
 Morricone/Yamaguchi: New Cinema Paradise Medley (1988)Demersseman: Fantai
 sie sur un thème original (1860)About the Artists  Originally from Palis
 ade\, Colorado\, Paul Lorenz currently lives in Fort Wayne\, Indiana\, wh
 ere he is lecturer of saxophone and assistant director of bands at Purdue
  University Fort Wayne (PFW). Lorenz is pursuing a Doctor of Music degree
  in Saxophone Performance at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Musi
 c\, where he served as associate instructor of saxophone. He previously e
 arned a Performer Diploma in Solo Performance at the Jacobs School and Ma
 ster of Music and Bachelor of Music degrees\, respectively\, from the Uni
 versity of Missouri—Kansas City and the University of Colorado at Bould
 er. His primary teachers include Otis Murphy\, Zachary Shemon\, and Tom M
 yer. Lorenz is active as both a solo performer and chamber musician and a
 lso teaches locally in the Fort Wayne area. He was recently featured as a
  soloist with the PFW Symphonic Wind Ensemble on its December concert as 
 well as at the 2023 IMEA Conference in Fort Wayne. He toured as soloist w
 ith the ensemble in Europe in May 2023. He was featured as guest soloist 
 with the South Shore Orchestra in a premier of a piece by George Kontogio
 rgos in summer 2024. In addition to his work at PFW\, he is the on-call s
 axophonist for the Fort Wayne Philharmonic Orchestra. Lorenz is the alto 
 saxophonist in Soma Quartet\, which formed at the Jacobs School of Music 
 in fall 2016. Soma has placed highly in numerous competitions including t
 he Fischoff National Chamber Music Competition\, Plowman Chamber Music Co
 mpetition\, Music Teachers National Association Competition\, NOLA Emergi
 ng Artists Chamber Competition (2021)\, Classics Alive Young Artist Compe
 tition\, and the Chicago Woodwind Ensemble Competition. Soma has performe
 d recitals at the International Navy Band Symposium\, the North American 
 Saxophone Alliance\, and was featured as part of a WFIU NPR broadcast in 
 2018.Angela Space is a classically trained musician and artist. Born in H
 artford\, she grew up in Windsor\, Connecticut\, and spent the majority o
 f her adult life in Oxford\, England. She has had a varied career in musi
 c. She was a music faculty member at several universities in the United S
 tates and UK with her longest tenure at the University of Southampton. Ou
 tside of academia\, Space has also taught music at Crisis UK\, Headington
  Rye Oxford School\, and at Her Majesty's Prison Springhill. Having earne
 d a Doctor of Musical Arts in Saxophone Performance\, Space has performed
  with the South Bend Symphony\, the Florida Philharmonic\, the New World 
 Symphony\, and the Temptations. Under the moniker Rainbow Reservoir she r
 ecorded two albums on Oddbox Records. Her band performed in venues and fe
 stivals across the UK and has been heard on BBC Radio 3 and 6 and BBC Rad
 io Oxford. As a fine artist\, Space works primarily in oils. She was acce
 pted into the 2025 Hoosier Art Salon and the 2023 Indiana Heritage Arts e
 xhibition and has exhibited work with Bloomington Portrait Group and Upla
 nd Plein Air Society. She is a member of IPAPA and the Portrait Society o
 f America. She has studied with Eric Jacobsen and Dale Zinkowski. Space c
 urrently divides her time between her home and studio in Indiana and her 
 place of solitude in Andalucia Spain.  Born in Japan\, Haruko Murphy move
 d with her family to San José\, California\, at age 14. She earned both 
 the Bachelor of Music and Master of Music degrees in Piano Performance\, 
 each with Highest Distinction\, from the Indiana University Jacobs School
  of Music\, where she studied under Distinguished Professor Menahem Press
 ler. She later continued her training at the Sweelinck Conservatorium in 
 Amsterdam as a pupil of Jan Huizing. A prizewinning solo pianist early in
  her career\, Murphy now enjoys an active life as a collaborative pianist
  and chamber musician. She specializes in the repertoire of the classical
  saxophone and has performed in concert with many internationally renowne
 d artists\, including Jean‑Yves Fourmeau\, Arno Bornkamp\, the Masato K
 umoi Saxophone Quartet\, and numerous others\, as well as her husband\, O
 tis Murphy. She is a frequent collaborative pianist at the Jacobs School.
 Otis Murphy is professor of saxophone at the Indiana University Jacobs Sc
 hool of Music\, where he joined the faculty at age 28\, becoming one of t
 he youngest professors in the school's history. Recognized internationall
 y for his artistry\, he has performed in more than 25 countries across fo
 ur continents\, appearing on distinguished stages including Carnegie Hall
  and Merkin Hall in New York\, Casals Hall in Tokyo\, and the Palau de la
  Música in Valencia. Murphy is a laureate in several national and intern
 ational competitions\, including the Adolphe Sax International Saxophone 
 Competition and the Jean‑Marie Londeix International Saxophone Competit
 ion. His discography includes numerous acclaimed recordings\, most notabl
 y his contribution to Pacifica Quartet's Contemporary Voices\, featuring 
 Ellen Taaffe Zwilich's Quintet for Alto Saxophone and String Quartet\, wh
 ich won a Grammy Award for Best Chamber Music/Small Ensemble Performance.
  Internationally respected as a pedagogue\, Murphy has led guest teaching
  engagements and master classes at major institutions such as the Paris C
 onservatory\, the Amsterdam Conservatory\, the Cologne Academy of Music\,
  the Royal College of Music in London\, and the Hamamatsu International W
 ind Instrument Academy. His students have garnered significant recognitio
 n\, earning awards in major competitions and holding professional appoint
 ments worldwide as performers\, educators\, and members of premier milita
 ry ensembles. Murphy lives in Bloomington\, Indiana\, with his wife\, pia
 nist Haruko Murphy. Together they share a vibrant performing partnership 
 and a joyful family life as devoted parents to six children.
