 cello/Pittel: <em>Oboe Concerto in C Minor\, S.Z799<br /></em>Piazzolla/I
 soda/Sugawa: <em>Selections from "Histoire du tango" (1985)<br /></em>Bea
 ch/Duncan: <em>Romance\, Op. 23 (1893)<br /></em>Ryota Ishikawa: <em>Bloo
 ming Ireland (2013)<br /></em>Morricone/Yamaguchi: <em>New Cinema Paradis
 e Medley (1988)<br /></em>Demersseman: <em>Fantaisie sur un thème origin
 al (1860)<br /></em><br /><strong>About the Artists</strong><br /><br /> 
  Originally from Palisade\, Colorado\, <strong>Paul Lorenz</strong> curre
 ntly lives in Fort Wayne\, Indiana\, where he is lecturer of saxophone an
 d assistant director of bands at Purdue University Fort Wayne (PFW). Lore
 nz is pursuing a Doctor of Music degree in Saxophone Performance at the I
 ndiana University Jacobs School of Music\, where he served as associate i
 nstructor of saxophone. He previously earned a Performer Diploma in Solo 
 Performance at the Jacobs School and Master of Music and Bachelor of Musi
 c degrees\, respectively\, from the University of Missouri—Kansas City 
 and the University of Colorado at Boulder. His primary teachers include O
 tis Murphy\, Zachary Shemon\, and Tom Myer. Lorenz is active as both a so
 lo performer and chamber musician and also teaches locally in the Fort Wa
 yne area. He was recently featured as a soloist with the PFW Symphonic Wi
 nd Ensemble on its December concert as well as at the 2023 IMEA Conferenc
 e in Fort Wayne. He toured as soloist with the ensemble in Europe in May 
 2023. He was featured as guest soloist with the South Shore Orchestra in 
 a premier of a piece by George Kontogiorgos in summer 2024. In addition t
 o his work at PFW\, he is the on-call saxophonist for the Fort Wayne Phil
 harmonic Orchestra. Lorenz is the alto saxophonist in Soma Quartet\, whic
 h formed at the Jacobs School of Music in fall 2016. Soma has placed high
 ly in numerous competitions including the Fischoff National Chamber Music
  Competition\, Plowman Chamber Music Competition\, Music Teachers Nationa
 l Association Competition\, NOLA Emerging Artists Chamber Competition (20
 21)\, Classics Alive Young Artist Competition\, and the Chicago Woodwind 
 Ensemble Competition. Soma has performed recitals at the International Na
 vy Band Symposium\, the North American Saxophone Alliance\, and was featu
 red as part of a WFIU NPR broadcast in 2018.<br /><br /><strong>Angela Sp
 ace</strong> is a classically trained musician and artist. Born in Hartfo
 rd\, she grew up in Windsor\, Connecticut\, and spent the majority of her
  adult life in Oxford\, England. She has had a varied career in music. Sh
 e was a music faculty member at several universities in the United States
  and UK with her longest tenure at the University of Southampton. Outside
  of academia\, Space has also taught music at Crisis UK\, Headington Rye 
 Oxford School\, and at Her Majesty's Prison Springhill. Having earned a D
 octor of Musical Arts in Saxophone Performance\, Space has performed with
  the South Bend Symphony\, the Florida Philharmonic\, the New World Symph
 ony\, and the Temptations. Under the moniker Rainbow Reservoir she record
 ed two albums on Oddbox Records. Her band performed in venues and festiva
 ls across the UK and has been heard on BBC Radio 3 and 6 and BBC Radio Ox
 ford. As a fine artist\, Space works primarily in oils. She was accepted 
 into the 2025 Hoosier Art Salon and the 2023 Indiana Heritage Arts exhibi
 tion and has exhibited work with Bloomington Portrait Group and Upland Pl
 ein Air Society. She is a member of IPAPA and the Portrait Society of Ame
 rica. She has studied with Eric Jacobsen and Dale Zinkowski. Space curren
 tly divides her time between her home and studio in Indiana and her place
  of solitude in Andalucia Spain.<br /><br />  Born in Japan\, <strong>Har
 uko Murphy</strong> moved with her family to San José\, California\, at 
 age 14. She earned both the Bachelor of Music and Master of Music degrees
  in Piano Performance\, each with Highest Distinction\, from the Indiana 
 University Jacobs School of Music\, where she studied under Distinguished
  Professor Menahem Pressler. She later continued her training at the Swee
 linck Conservatorium in Amsterdam as a pupil of Jan Huizing. A prizewinni
 ng solo pianist early in her career\, Murphy now enjoys an active life as
  a collaborative pianist and chamber musician. She specializes in the rep
 ertoire of the classical saxophone and has performed in concert with many
  internationally renowned artists\, including Jean‑Yves Fourmeau\, Arno
  Bornkamp\, the Masato Kumoi Saxophone Quartet\, and numerous others\, as
  well as her husband\, Otis Murphy. She is a frequent collaborative piani
 st at the Jacobs School.<br /><br /><strong>Otis Murphy</strong> is profe
 ssor of saxophone at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music\, wher
 e he joined the faculty at age 28\, becoming one of the youngest professo
 rs in the school's history. Recognized internationally for his artistry\,
  he has performed in more than 25 countries across four continents\, appe
 aring on distinguished stages including Carnegie Hall and Merkin Hall in 
 New York\, Casals Hall in Tokyo\, and the Palau de la Música in Valencia
 . Murphy is a laureate in several national and international competitions
 \, including the Adolphe Sax International Saxophone Competition and the 
 Jean‑Marie Londeix International Saxophone Competition. His discography
  includes numerous acclaimed recordings\, most notably his contribution t
 o Pacifica Quartet's <em>Contemporary Voices</em>\, featuring Ellen Taaff
 e Zwilich's Quintet for Alto Saxophone and String Quartet\, which won a G
 rammy Award for Best Chamber Music/Small Ensemble Performance. Internatio
 nally respected as a pedagogue\, Murphy has led guest teaching engagement
 s and master classes at major institutions such as the Paris Conservatory
 \, the Amsterdam Conservatory\, the Cologne Academy of Music\, the Royal 
 College of Music in London\, and the Hamamatsu International Wind Instrum
 ent Academy. His students have garnered significant recognition\, earning
  awards in major competitions and holding professional appointments world
 wide as performers\, educators\, and members of premier military ensemble
 s. Murphy lives in Bloomington\, Indiana\, with his wife\, pianist Haruko
  Murphy. Together they share a vibrant performing partnership and a joyfu
 l family life as devoted parents to six children.</p>
