LOCATION:Auer Hall &amp\; LIVE@jacobs
SUMMARY:José Ramón Méndez\, piano
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy this performance from almost anywhere in the world viaL
 IVE@jacobs!Repertoire  J. S. Bach/Busoni: Ich ruf' zu dir\, Herr Jesu Chr
 ist\, BWV 639J. Haydn: Variations in F Minor\, Hob.XVII:6Medtner: Fairy T
 ale\, Op. 20 No. 1 (1909)Medtner: 'Sonata tragica' from "Forgotten Melodi
 es No. 2\, Op. 39" (1920)Beach: Gavotte fantastique\, Op. 54 No. 2 (1903)
 Scriabin: Selections from "Ten Mazurkas\, Op. 3" (1890)Poulenc: Trois nov
 elettes (1959)Sibelius: Finlandia\, Op. 26 (1899/1900)About the Artist  D
 escribed as "an artist with a polished sound and tremendous constructive 
 power" and hailed by the Hoja del Lunes de Madrid as "the Spanish pianist
  of his generation\," José Ramón Méndez is one of the most exciting Sp
 anish pianists of today. Recent performances include Chopin's first piano
  concerto with the Cuban National Symphony Orchestra and Manuel de Falla'
 s Nights in the Gardens of Spain with the Nittany Valley Symphony as well
  as numerous solo and chamber music performances in the United States\, E
 urope\, and Asia. His playing has been featured on APM's Performance Toda
 y and on Classical KMFA. He has been a guest performer at many music fest
 ivals\, including Caramoor Festival\, Barge Music Series\, Festival Inter
 nacional de Piano de las Islas Canarias\, and Amalfi Coast International 
 Music Festival\, to name a few. As a chamber musician\, Méndez has colla
 borated with such distinguished artists as Karl Leister\, Itzhak Perlman\
 , and Pinchas Zukerman. Méndez received his first music instruction from
  his father and by the age of seven was performing on Spanish television 
 and radio stations. He made his solo debut at age 11 at the Oviedo Philha
 rmonic Society (Spain). He first gained international recognition when he
  performed Liszt's first piano concerto under the direction of Sergiu Com
 missiona at the Concertgebouw in Amsterdam. Since then\, he has concertiz
 ed extensively in his native Spain\, the United States\, Italy\, England\
 , Portugal\, Holland\, and Japan to great acclaim. Méndez earned bachelo
 r's\, master's\, and doctoral degrees from the Manhattan School of Music\
 , where he studied with Solomon Mikowsky\, Byron Janis\, and Miyoko Lotto
 . He has won top prizes in many international competitions\, including Pi
 lar Bayona International Piano Competition\, Hilton Head Island Internati
 onal Piano Competition\, and Frederick Chopin Competition in New York\, a
 mong others. Formerly on the piano faculty at University of Texas at Aust
 in\, New York University\, Pennsylvania State University\, Northwestern U
 niversity\, and University of Northern Arizona\, Flagstaff\, he is curren
 tly associate professor of piano at Oberlin Conservatory. Méndez is a Ya
 maha Artist.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Enjoy this performance from almos
 t anywhere in the world via</strong><a href="https://liveatjacobs.music.i
 ndiana.edu/"><strong>LIVE@jacobs</strong></a><strong>!</strong><br /><br 
 /><strong>Repertoire</strong><br />  J. S. Bach/Busoni: <em>Ich ruf' zu d
 ir\, Herr Jesu Christ\, BWV 639<br /></em>J. Haydn: <em>Variations in F M
 inor\, Hob.XVII:6<br /></em>Medtner: <em>Fairy Tale\, Op. 20 No. 1 (1909)
 <br /></em>Medtner: <em>'Sonata tragica' from "Forgotten Melodies No. 2\,
  Op. 39" (1920)<br /></em>Beach: <em>Gavotte fantastique\, Op. 54 No. 2 (
 1903)<br /></em>Scriabin: <em>Selections from "Ten Mazurkas\, Op. 3" (189
 0)<br /></em>Poulenc: <em>Trois novelettes (1959)<br /></em>Sibelius: <em
 >Finlandia\, Op. 26 (1899/1900)</em><br /><br /><strong>About the Artist<
 /strong><br /><br />  Described as "an artist with a polished sound and t
 remendous constructive power" and hailed by the <em>Hoja del Lunes de Mad
 rid</em> as "the Spanish pianist of his generation\," <strong>José Ramó
 n Méndez</strong> is one of the most exciting Spanish pianists of today.
  Recent performances include Chopin's first piano concerto with the Cuban
  National Symphony Orchestra and Manuel de Falla's <em>Nights in the Gard
 ens of Spain</em> with the Nittany Valley Symphony as well as numerous so
 lo and chamber music performances in the United States\, Europe\, and Asi
 a. His playing has been featured on APM's <em>Performance Today</em> and 
 on Classical KMFA. He has been a guest performer at many music festivals\
 , including Caramoor Festival\, Barge Music Series\, Festival Internacion
 al de Piano de las Islas Canarias\, and Amalfi Coast International Music 
 Festival\, to name a few. As a chamber musician\, Méndez has collaborate
 d with such distinguished artists as Karl Leister\, Itzhak Perlman\, and 
 Pinchas Zukerman. Méndez received his first music instruction from his f
 ather and by the age of seven was performing on Spanish television and ra
 dio stations. He made his solo debut at age 11 at the Oviedo Philharmonic
  Society (Spain). He first gained international recognition when he perfo
 rmed Liszt's first piano concerto under the direction of Sergiu Commissio
 na at the Concertgebouw in Amsterdam. Since then\, he has concertized ext
 ensively in his native Spain\, the United States\, Italy\, England\, Port
 ugal\, Holland\, and Japan to great acclaim. Méndez earned bachelor's\, 
 master's\, and doctoral degrees from the Manhattan School of Music\, wher
 e he studied with Solomon Mikowsky\, Byron Janis\, and Miyoko Lotto. He h
 as won top prizes in many international competitions\, including Pilar Ba
 yona International Piano Competition\, Hilton Head Island International P
 iano Competition\, and Frederick Chopin Competition in New York\, among o
 thers. Formerly on the piano faculty at University of Texas at Austin\, N
 ew York University\, Pennsylvania State University\, Northwestern Univers
 ity\, and University of Northern Arizona\, Flagstaff\, he is currently as
 sociate professor of piano at Oberlin Conservatory. Méndez is a Yamaha A
 rtist.</p>
